From f0885769cf409e498d43ea99cd50eed4d9114e76 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 18:15:24 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0216 -- keep a played clip's decoded PCM, not
 only its bytes

The sound cache keeps a clip's file bytes, so every play of a clip -- a
looping ambient, a repeated footstep or chime -- decoded, resampled and
converted it again on the main thread. Room 35 of the S8 fixture re-decodes
one clip every ~0.85 s: 107 decodes of ~14 ms in ~90 s (LAB-JOURNEYS 9b.2),
each one a hitch in play.

A decoded clip is now one immutable object (device-format samples, their
count, their format), shared by every decoder playing it and by a cache.
The cache is keyed by the sound cache's own byte buffer, which every play
of a cached clip receives as the same shared_ptr: an entry holds it by a
weak reference and hits only while that buffer is alive and is the one
asked about, so it can never answer for other bytes and it lapses with
the sound cache's entry -- no asset names needed. Streamed clips (no
buffer) are never cached. LRU under DOSAGS_PCM_CACHE_KB (default 2048, the
sound cache's size; 0 = off); a clip larger than the cap is not cached.
DOSAGS_PCM_CACHE_VERIFY=1 also decodes every hit afresh and counts
differences, for a witness cell. RUNMANI.LOG gets "DOS-PORT pcmcache
hits= misses= evictions= uncacheable= lapsed= entries= bytes= peak_bytes=
cap_kb= verify= verify_mismatches=".

The shared object replaces _pcm (the 16-bit intermediate, kept after the
device conversion only for its sample count) and _pcmDevice, so a playing
clip is also held once instead of twice.
